Subject: [PATCH] Do not close the listening socket when configuring an accepted
 socket fails

WebSocketServer.doAccept configures a freshly accepted socket (setTcpNoDelay,
setKeepAlive) outside any try/catch of its own, and declares `throws
IOException`. On a BSD-family host, setsockopt on a socket whose peer has
already sent RST returns EINVAL, which the JDK surfaces as SocketException. It
unwinds out of doAccept into the selector loop's last resort:

    } catch (IOException ex) {
      handleIOException(key, null, ex);
    }

where `key` is the SERVER's acceptable key. handleIOException cancels it and,
having no WebSocket to blame, closes key.channel() -- the listening channel.
The server object, its selector thread and its existing connections all stay
healthy; it simply stops accepting, and the port is unbound. Nothing reports
it: handleIOException only log.trace()s, and onError is handleFatal's path,
not this one.

Attribute the failure to the connection it came from instead.

--- a/src/main/java/org/java_websocket/server/WebSocketServer.java
+++ b/src/main/java/org/java_websocket/server/WebSocketServer.java
@@
     SocketChannel channel = server.accept();
     if (channel == null) {
       return;
     }
-    channel.configureBlocking(false);
-    Socket socket = channel.socket();
-    socket.setTcpNoDelay(isTcpNoDelay());
-    socket.setKeepAlive(true);
+    try {
+      channel.configureBlocking(false);
+      Socket socket = channel.socket();
+      socket.setTcpNoDelay(isTcpNoDelay());
+      socket.setKeepAlive(true);
+    } catch (IOException ex) {
+      // This failure belongs to the connection we just accepted, not to the
+      // server. Letting it out of doAccept sends it to the selector loop's
+      // last-resort handleIOException(key, null, ex), where `key` is the
+      // server's own acceptable key -- which cancels that key and closes the
+      // listening channel, taking the whole server off the air silently.
+      log.trace("Could not configure an accepted socket; closing that connection", ex);
+      try {
+        channel.close();
+      } catch (IOException ignored) {
+        // nothing useful to do; the connection is being discarded anyway
+      }
+      return;
+    }
     WebSocketImpl w = wsf.createWebSocket(this, drafts);
     w.setSelectionKey(channel.register(selector, SelectionKey.OP_READ, w));
     try {
